{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean head 31ddeb38f8449f88bb9f8d2422572045a527fb20, artifact f3e0610627f951fee380881b6e7e12274d3c727d.\n\nThe requested distinction is correct and fail-closed. A named test that explicitly reports failure produces fail; a skipped or never-reported test produces error because the property was not established; run or decode errors also produce error; and an explicit property failure outranks a simultaneous harness error. Both non-pass outcomes still exit non-zero. The distinction reaches console details, stable Markdown, and run-specific evidence. A passing make spike remains quiet apart from its existing success line and regenerates a clean tracked projection.\n\nThe intermittent diagnosis satisfies the request. Git history confirms that before c9dc187d the reporter ran go test ./..., so an unrelated suite failure set runErr while all six named cases could remain pass. c9dc187d replaced that broad lane with per-package named selections before this head. The exact historical failing test cannot now be recovered because the runner-local evidence was discarded; this head closes that evidence-loss defect rather than inventing a cause.\n\nThirty-day upload is proportionate. The workflow passes no secrets, checks out without stored credentials, and uploads only spike/.spike on failure. That directory contains the named test evidence and captured runner output needed to diagnose the red lane; limiting retention to thirty days avoids indefinite publication while supporting comparison of repeats.\n\nArchitecture: this affects the Workroom-adjacent adversarial evidence tool and CI surface only. It changes no kernel, application fold, projection, durable protocol, connector, or UI contract; the kernel-application boundary is preserved and architecture.md need not move.\n\nSecurity: the lane remains red whenever evidence is absent or a property fails, a real security failure cannot be softened by a runner error, and failed-run evidence